{"text":"Recent work supports integrating textual and collaborative representations, but does not establish the exact combination of scratch-trained text encoding, BERT4Rec, and reliable semantic hard negatives. TedRec (CIKM 2024) learns sequence-level text–ID fusion; CLA-Rec (ICDM 2024) aligns LLM-derived description embeddings with collaborative information. Both motivate learning task-specific relationships rather than equating textual similarity with recommendation relevance. Their results do not demonstrate that a lightweight encoder trained without external weights will provide equivalent benefits. [TedRec](https://github.com/RUCAIBox/TedRec/blob/main/README.md), [CLA-Rec](https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10884256/)

Negative-sampling research warns that the hardest unobserved items can be plausible positives. The 2026 negative-sampling survey identifies false negatives, biased gradients, and unstable optimization as central problems. SID4SRec (2026) explicitly filters semantically overlapping negatives at sequence and item levels; however, its attribute-guided component uses category/brand information unavailable in this task. Semantic filtering is therefore relevant evidence, rather than a directly transferable implementation. [Survey](https://doi.org/10.1145/3793855), [SID4SRec](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0950705126010221)

A constraint-compatible experimental hypothesis: jointly train a small token-embedding encoder with pooling or shallow convolutions over supplied titles/descriptions, integrate its representation with item IDs inside one BERT4Rec, and retain masked-target training and appended-mask inference. Mix uniform negatives with moderately difficult candidates; exclude known training positives and cautiously downweight suspected false negatives. These are proposed adaptations, not established findings. Broad textual similarity alone cannot establish compatibility or negative validity; validate text integration and sampling separately using the fixed macro validation HR@10."}
